# Close out the current issue

Run this when implementation is done and you are ready to land the work (commit, push, PR). Post-merge branch hygiene now lives in a separate command, `/iflow-cleanup`, which you run **after** the PR is merged.

{% include "skills/_model_directive.md.j2" %}

{% include "skills/_resolve_project_root.md.j2" %}

## Input

Optional text after the command (same line). Examples:

- **No extra text** — close the issue without bumping the package version.
- **`bump`** (no level) — bump using the **pre-release-aware default**: stay on the current release channel (alpha→alpha, beta→beta, rc→rc, dev→dev) or **patch** when the current version is already stable. See `iflow-version-bump`.
- **A named level** — `patch`, `minor`, `major`, `stable`, `alpha`, `beta`, `rc`, `post`, or `dev` (e.g. `bump minor`, `beta`) → use exactly that `uv version --bump <level>`. `dev` must be paired with another component (e.g. `bump patch dev`).
- **Free text** — if it clearly asks to release or bump the version, infer the level from wording (e.g. "bugfix release" → `patch`, "promote to beta" → `beta`); if unclear, ask once. Never auto-pick `major`.
- **`nohistory`** or **`skip history`** — skip the `{{ history_file }}` update step (step 3) for this run.
- **`log "..."`** or **`note "..."`** — use the quoted text verbatim as the `{{ history_file }}` bullet summary (otherwise the GitHub issue title is used).
- **`stay`**, **`stay on branch`**, **`don't switch`**, or **`dont switch to main`** — after the PR step, stay on the issue branch instead of switching back to the default branch.
- **`yolo`** — hands-off close (used by `/iflow-yolo`): decide the `{{ history_file }}` update yourself without a confirm prompt (step 3), **merge the PR** after opening it (step 7a), then switch back to the default branch and pull (step 8). See the per-step notes below.

Other optional notes still apply: branch name, PR title, draft PR, skip issue doc update, commit all changes, etc.

## Typical steps

1. **Sanity check**
   - Run tests and any checks you rely on (e.g. `uv run pytest`).
   - **Ruff (when present).** If the project uses ruff (`[tool.ruff]` in `pyproject.toml`, ruff in dev dependencies, or `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/python-quality-tools.md` exists), run auto-fix lint through the documented Python runner before committing — e.g. `uv run ruff check --fix …` then `uv run ruff format …` (match paths to what the project documents).
   - Skim the diff so the commit matches what you intend to ship.
   - Confirm that any design decisions or good-practices that emerged from this issue are captured under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/` before committing. If something is missing, add it now (short markdown: context, decision, alternatives, link back to the issue).
   - **Graph freshness (optional).** If this change touched the project's structure (new modules, big refactor, removed files) and `graphify-out/` exists, suggest the user run `/iflow-graphify` (default: `graphify update`, AST-only, no LLM key needed) before pushing so teammates pull a current `GRAPH_REPORT.md`. Do not run `/iflow-graphify` automatically — it is opt-in. Skip this bullet entirely if `graphify-out/` is not present.

2. **Optional version bump** (only if the user asked for it in the command input)
   - Read `{{ agent_dir }}/skills/iflow-version-bump/SKILL.md` and follow it — it resolves the project's **release strategy** first (the "Release & version bump" section of `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/this-project.md`, else `pyproject.toml` detection, else the uv default), and defines every supported level and the pre-release-aware default.
   - **Static version:** from the project root, run `uv version --bump <level>` for the resolved level (`bump` with no level → the default rule based on the current version; or the named level from the input). Do this **before** updating issue markdown and **before** commit / push / PR so the new version is in the tree that gets merged.
   - **Git-tag derived:** edit nothing — compute and report the **planned tag** (next version from the latest tag, e.g. `v1.0.4a3`), record it in the status file, and defer creating the tag until after the merge (step 8 with `yolo`, else `/iflow-cleanup`). Never tag the issue branch.
   - If neither strategy applies (no `pyproject.toml` version at all), skip and say so; continue with the remaining steps.

3. **Update `{{ history_file }}`** (opt-out via `nohistory`)
   - Read `{{ agent_dir }}/skills/iflow-history-update/SKILL.md` and follow it.
   - Default summary for the new bullet is the GitHub issue title; override with `log "..."` / `note "..."` from the command input.
   - If step 2 did **not** bump (or plan) a version, append a bullet to the existing `## [Unreleased]` section: `- <summary>. (#<N>)`.
   - If step 2 **did** bump or plan a version, promote `## [Unreleased]` to `## [<new_version>] - <YYYY-MM-DD>` (for tag-derived projects use the planned tag's version), prepend a fresh empty `## [Unreleased]` above it, and put the new bullet inside the just-closed release section.
   - If `{{ history_file }}` does not exist at the project root, skip this step with a short note and continue. Never create it here.
   - Show the proposed diff and confirm once before writing. If declined, leave `{{ history_file }}` untouched and move on.
   - **`yolo` input:** do not ask — decide yourself and write the bullet (issue title, or the `log "..."` text) directly. `nohistory` still skips the step entirely.

4. **Issue tracking in the repo** (see project rules under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}`)
   - Update the status file for this issue: clear checklist, remaining work, and use `- [x] Done` only when fully resolved.
   - If the issue is fully resolved, move its markdown files from `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}` to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ solved_folder }}`. If partially resolved, move to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ partly_solved_folder }}`.

5. **Commit and fix merge conflicts**
   - Before staging, run `git status` to list all modified/untracked files. If any changes are **not relevant** to this issue, tell the user which ones and ask whether to include them in this commit or leave them for later. Do not silently drop or include unrelated changes.
   - Unless told to commit all, stage the right files (avoid unrelated changes). Include `pyproject.toml` (and `uv.lock` if it changed) when a version bump ran. Include `{{ history_file }}` when step 3 updated it.
   - Write a commit message that states what changed and why in normal sentences.
   - Sync with the default branch before pushing: run `git fetch --prune` then `git pull --ff-only` from the default branch (e.g. `main`) merged into the issue branch (or rebase, per project preference). Use `--ff-only` so unrelated work never gets merged in silently; if it refuses, stop and ask how to reconcile. Check for and fix merge conflicts.

6. **Push**
   - Push your branch to `origin` (or the remote you use).

7. **Pull request**
   - Open a PR against the default branch (e.g. `main`).
   - Describe the change, how to test it, and link the GitHub issue (e.g. `Closes #123` or `Refs #123` in the PR body).

7a. **Merge the PR (`yolo` input only)**
   - Merge immediately with `gh pr merge <number> --squash` (squash matches the project's assume-squash-merge convention). Never pass `--delete-branch` — branch deletion stays in `/iflow-cleanup`.
   - If GitHub refuses (branch protection, required checks still running), fall back to `gh pr merge <number> --squash --auto` and report that the merge is queued behind the checks.
   - If even `--auto` fails, stop the hands-off behaviour there: report the error and leave the PR open for the user.
   - Without the `yolo` input, skip this step — merging stays a user decision (step 9).

8. **Switch back when safe**
   - If the input included `stay`, `stay on branch`, `don't switch`, or `dont switch to main`, stay on the issue branch and report that the user opted out.
   - **CLI fast path (preferred).** If the `issue-flow` CLI is on `PATH`, run `issue-flow agent switchback --json` after the PR is open or updated. It performs this whole step deterministically: refuses while the working tree is dirty (listing the paths), else switches to the detected default branch and runs `git pull --ff-only`. On exit 1, report its `notes` to the user and stop — do not force anything.
   - **Manual fallback.** Detect the default branch: prefer `gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef -q .defaultBranchRef.name`, else `git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's|^origin/||'`, else `main`. Run `git status --porcelain`; if it is clean, switch back with `git switch <default>` and then run `git pull --ff-only`. A clean tree here means the branch work has been committed and pushed to the PR branch. If the working tree is dirty, stay on the current branch, list the uncommitted paths, and explain that switching is unsafe until those changes are committed, stashed, or discarded by the user.
   - **`yolo` input:** this step is mandatory (unless `stay` was also passed) and runs **after** the merge from step 7a, so the pull brings the just-merged squash commit into the local default branch. If step 7a only queued an auto-merge, still switch and pull, and note that the merge commit will arrive once checks pass.
   - **Planned release tag (`yolo` + tag-derived strategy only):** if step 2 planned a tag, create it now — after the pull, standing on the squash commit — with `git tag <planned>` then `git push origin <planned>` (covered by the yolo consolidated confirm). If the merge was only queued via `--auto`, leave the tag to `/iflow-cleanup` and say so.
   - This step never deletes the issue branch. Branch deletion remains in `/iflow-cleanup`.

9. **After review**
   - With the `yolo` input the PR was already merged in step 7a — skip to the `/iflow-cleanup` reminder below.
   - Address feedback, push updates, and merge when approved and CI is green.
   - If step 8 switched back to the default branch, switch to the PR branch again before making review fixes.
   - Once the PR is merged, run **`/iflow-cleanup`** to switch back to the default branch, `git pull --ff-only`, `git fetch --prune`, and delete local branches whose commits are already in the default branch (single consolidated confirm — which, for tag-derived projects, also offers to create the release tag planned in step 2). `/iflow-close` no longer does post-merge cleanup itself.

## Output

Summarize what was committed, pushed, the PR URL (or next step if blocked), and whether the working copy switched back to the default branch or stayed on the issue branch. With the `yolo` input, also report the merge result (merged, or queued via `--auto`). Remind the user to run `/iflow-cleanup` after the PR merges (or right away when yolo already merged it).
